lines=['# P06 rib-to-saddle connection review','', '**Detailed geometry candidate. Peel/fatigue and wing-slot reinforcement remain unresolved.**','', '## Connection modeled','', 'Eight external glass/epoxy splice plates bridge the P05 rib/saddle gaps. Front plates are2 mm thick, rear plates1.5 mm. Each overlaps24 mm along the arm, bonds to the saddle wall below and to a fitted1.8 mm rib packer above. Three explicit0.2 mm bond layers per connection make the lap interfaces visible and measurable. The rib-to-spar/skin connection remains a wing-group task.','',f"Added {len(g['added_components'])} separately identified pieces/layers; arm-only STEP contains {g['STEP']['native_solids']} solids. Existing P05 motors, motor mounts and carbon arms are retained. Native fixed joints attach ribs through the new front splice, with a nonredundant constraint tree. Rear physical bond paths are checked geometrically without adding redundant fixed-joint loops.",'','Original P05 minimum rib/saddle gaps:0.997/1.454 mm at the front and2.964/3.336 mm at the rear on the positive-Y arm. Flat plates bridge those spaces; the design does not depend on edge contact.','', '## Verified geometry and explicit wing changes','',f"- Internal new-part interference pairs: {len(g['physical_collisions'])}.",f"- Native reopen/solver and face coverage: {v['status']}.",f"- {v['bond_interface_groups']} bond groups, with48 adherend-side contact checks. Minimum face coverage / mean slab area: {min(x['minimum_coverage_ratio'] for x in v['coverage'])*100:.2f}%. Joint screens use the smaller measured contact area, then an assumed50% effective-area factor.",f"- Arm-only STEP passes geometry, solid count and component-center checks; maximum component-center error {g['STEP']['max_center_error_mm']:.6g} mm.",'- Spar outside-surface clearance:0.4 mm. Native conical outer surface is filled and offset, including the inner bore region; no spar material is removed.','- Proposed lower-skin entry slots: four per arm, eight total. Length24.6 mm, width4.8 mm front/4.3 mm rear. The skin cutter stops atZ7 mm. Upper plate contours are trimmed to the actual core envelope with0.3 mm vertical margin; upper-skin interference is checked separately.','- Post-relief skin clearance:0.3 mm nominal, with no detected remaining physical intersection. Foam context includes installation clearance. These are proposed skin/foam cutouts, not a released wing modification.','', '## Skin quantity limitation','', 'The relieved wing-skin shapes are valid and pass the geometric clearance test, but their Boolean volumes fail conservation. The raw differences include an unphysical apparent volume increase on one side. **No skin cutout mass credit is accepted.** The original P05 skin quantities stay in the budget. Diagnostic quantities remain in geometry_checks.json; valid BREP alone is not accepted as mass validation.','', 'Foam removed volumes pass the separate conservation check (within1% of the removed volume). Only that foam credit is used. Skin-slot reinforcement/sealing mass is not designed or credited yet.','', '## Mass reconciliation','',f"- P05 structural budget: {old['structural_budget_kg']*1000:.3f} g.",f"- P06 provisional gross structural budget: {m['structural_budget_kg']*1000:.3f} g.",f"- Increase: {r['structural_delta_kg']*1000:.3f} g.",f"- New glass laminate: {r['new_glass_kg']*1000:.3f} g; explicit new epoxy: {r['new_epoxy_kg']*1000:.3f} g.",f"- Epoxy is reallocated from the existing saddle/rib adhesive budget: {r['adhesive_residual_before_kg']*1000:.3f} → {r['adhesive_residual_after_kg']*1000:.3f} g residual. It is not counted twice.",f"- Foam credit: {r['foam_mass_credit_kg']*1000:.4f} g. Skin credit:0.000 g.",f"- Accounted / sizing aircraft: {m['accounted_aircraft_kg']:.4f} / {m['sizing_aircraft_kg']:.4f} kg. Reserve remains200 g, separate and counted once.",f"- Aircraft CG X: {m['aircraft_cg_m'][0]*1000:.2f} mm, provisional.",'- Earlier71.683 g generic hardware and42 g embedded mount allowances remain removed. Motor/prop mass rows and battery budget remain unchanged. Future wing-slot reinforcement and other existing P04/P05 gaps are not included.','', '## Preliminary demand checks','', '- Inherited hypothetical17.3 N/motor ×1.5 gives25.95 N/motor. Front-only/rear-only cases reverse the saddle reactions; peak absolute front/rear reaction is84.725/96.151 N.','- Both equal rib sharing and a full station reaction carried by one splice are evaluated. Strength values, load sharing and effective-area reduction remain assumptions.','- Front plate net width is sampled across the actual spar-relief section atZ5–11 mm in0.25 mm increments. Full one-splice force is conservatively applied across the smallest sampled section. Rear full-width bridge section is used. This is a strip approximation, not a laminate/3D stress solution.',f"- Worst average-shear utilization: {max(x['shear_utilization'] for x in j):.3f} against an assumed2 MPa value.",f"- Worst combined plate-strip utilization: {max(x['plate_utilization'] for x in j):.3f} against an assumed70 MPa value.",f"- Maximum adhesive peel-demand proxy: {max(x['peel_demand_proxy_MPa'] for x in j):.3f} MPa. No qualified peel capacity is available, so this remains UNRESOLVED.",'- Eccentricity, spar-relief load distribution, laminate interlaminar failure, saddle-wall crippling, bond end effects, hot/wet exposure and fatigue require refined analysis and representative joint tests. Passing the two preliminary averages does not qualify the connection.','', '## Global wing screens','']
